<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Périgord</h1><p class="st-meta">866 CE – 1399 CE (approx.) · capital Périgueux</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Medieval county of the Dordogne valley around Périgueux, the land of the ancient Petrocorii.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 866 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> entrusted the counties of Périgord and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vulgrin-i-count-of-angouleme">Vulgrin</a>, a kinsman, to resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids along the rivers of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After his death the two counties were divided between his sons, and Périgord passed to a line of counts from whom the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand-Périgord</a> family claimed descent.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine</a> and held a land of castles and abbeys on the frontier between English and French power. Their authority was contested by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perigueux">Périgueux</a>, whose town of Puy-Saint-Front won wide privileges.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> the counts changed sides repeatedly. In 1399 Count Archambaud VI was condemned for treason and his county confiscated; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI</a> gave it in 1400 to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-duke-of-orleans">Louis of Orléans</a>. It later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-albret">Albret</a> family and was united with the crown under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-of-perigord.jpg" alt="County of Périgord" width="320" height="382"><figcaption>Image: Reigen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_1154-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>866 CE – 1399 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Périgueux</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Vulgrin, Archambaud VI</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Périgord</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1132&amp;focus=county-of-perigord" aria-label="County of Périgord on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4696 582 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Périgord lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5016 838L5035 888M5016 838L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5035 888h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5016 838h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5016 838h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1132&amp;focus=county-of-perigord">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

